<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Loops on iOS UIKit Course</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/</link><description>Recent content in Loops on iOS UIKit Course</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><atom:link href="/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>5.1. For loops</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.1.-for-loops/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.1.-for-loops/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="51-for-loops"&gt;5.1. For loops&lt;a class="anchor" href="#51-for-loops"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For loops are the most common loops in Swift, and you&amp;rsquo;ll probably use this loop for more than 90% of cases. We use &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt; loops to iterate over a sequence of values or a range of values.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For example, the following code prints the integers in the range &lt;code&gt;1...10&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; range = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1.&lt;/span&gt;..&lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; number &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; range{
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(number)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Another example could be iterating through an array and printing the elements:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>5.2. While loops</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.2.-while-loops/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.2.-while-loops/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="52-while-loops"&gt;5.2. While loops&lt;a class="anchor" href="#52-while-loops"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The second most common loops in Swift are &lt;code&gt;while&lt;/code&gt; loops. We use a while loop to run a code block repeatedly as long as a given condition is true. A general structure of a &lt;code&gt;while&lt;/code&gt; loop is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;while&lt;/span&gt; condition {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//code block&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;For example, the following code prints all the integers below 50:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; targetNum = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;while&lt;/span&gt; targetNum &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;{
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//code block&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(targetNum)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; targetNum &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here the condition is: if &lt;code&gt;targetNum&lt;/code&gt; is less than or equal to 50.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>5.3. Repeat loops</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.3.-repeat-loops/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.3.-repeat-loops/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="53-repeat-loops"&gt;5.3. Repeat loops&lt;a class="anchor" href="#53-repeat-loops"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The third way of writing loops is &lt;code&gt;repeat&lt;/code&gt; loops. It is identical to a while loop, but the condition is checked at the end of the code block. If we use the same example from before, we can write:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; myNum = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;repeat&lt;/span&gt;{
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;//code block&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(myNum)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; myNum &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;} &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;while&lt;/span&gt; myNum &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;The point is &lt;code&gt;repeat&lt;/code&gt; loops will execute the code block at least once. So, you should be careful about the cases where the first iteration may result in errors.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>5.4. Break and continue</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.4.-break-and-continue/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.4.-break-and-continue/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="54-break-and-continue"&gt;5.4. Break and continue&lt;a class="anchor" href="#54-break-and-continue"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h3 id="breakingexiting-loops"&gt;Breaking/exiting loops&lt;a class="anchor" href="#breakingexiting-loops"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sometimes we want to stop repeating the code block in a loop. We use the keyword &lt;code&gt;break&lt;/code&gt; to do that. Let&amp;rsquo;s look into the example below:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-swift" data-lang="swift"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; breakPoint = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; countDown = &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;while&lt;/span&gt; breakPoint &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; {
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;I will run!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; 
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#75715e"&gt;// activating break point&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; countDown == breakPoint{
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; print(&lt;span style="color:#e6db74"&gt;&amp;#34;I am tired now!&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#66d9ef"&gt;break&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; }
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt; countDown &lt;span style="color:#f92672"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style="color:#ae81ff"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here the while loop is running infinitely if the value of &lt;code&gt;breakPoint&lt;/code&gt; is greater than or equal to &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt;. So, we need to break it when the &lt;code&gt;countDown&lt;/code&gt; reaches the &lt;code&gt;breakPoint&lt;/code&gt;. Thats where we user the keyword &lt;code&gt;break&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>5.5. Reference Code</title><link>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.5.-reference-code/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>/docs/02-swift-fundamentals/loops/5.5.-reference-code/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="55-reference-code"&gt;5.5. Reference Code&lt;a class="anchor" href="#55-reference-code"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
